3
00:00:10.331 --> 00:00:14.440
weights, an auditor may need to verify the executed artifact,

4
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:20.482
and every party may need output integrity without granting another party

5
00:00:20.482 --> 00:00:25.148
unrestricted custody. Ordinary transport encryption ends before computation begins.

6
00:00:25.148 --> 00:00:30.331
The tempting shortcut is insufficient: Privacy policy governs permitted information

7
00:00:30.331 --> 00:00:33.960
use, while hardware custody governs stored artifacts.

8
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:39.718
Neither supplies a guarantee-by-guarantee execution contract covering input

9
00:00:39.718 --> 00:00:44.459
confidentiality, model confidentiality, computation integrity, output authenticity,

10
00:00:44.459 --> 00:00:50.555
attestation freshness, leakage, verifier policy, cost, and failure recovery.

11
00:00:51.155 --> 00:00:57.345
The chapter's core claim is this: Confidential and verifiable AI requires a

12
00:00:57.345 --> 00:01:01.988
compositional execution contract that names the adversary, protected assets,

13
00:01:01.988 --> 00:01:07.147
permitted leakage, trust anchors, proof or attestation statement, verifier policy,

14
00:01:07.147 --> 00:01:09.210
freshness, revocation, performance budget,

15
00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:15.874
and authorization boundary; no primitive or attestation may be treated as proof of

16
00:01:15.874 --> 00:01:19.280
semantic correctness, legitimate purpose, or end-to-end privacy.

17
00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:26.498
Express the requested guarantee vector separately for input privacy, model privacy,

18
00:01:26.498 --> 00:01:29.624
intermediate-state privacy, computation integrity, output

19
00:01:29.624 --> 00:01:32.125
authenticity, availability, and auditability.

20
00:01:32.465 --> 00:01:37.003
Choose among FHE, MPC, zero-knowledge proofs, confidential execution,

21
00:01:37.003 --> 00:01:42.676
private retrieval, differential privacy, or hybrid composition against an explicit

22
00:01:42.676 --> 00:01:44.945
adversary and leakage model.

23
00:01:45.545 --> 00:01:49.575
A concrete implementation trace makes the proposal testable.

24
00:01:49.915 --> 00:01:56.569
A guarantee matrix and local demonstration for one bounded inference operation with

25
00:01:56.569 --> 00:02:01.559
explicit adversary and leakage models, artifact and configuration commitments,

26
00:02:01.559 --> 00:02:04.332
freshness, independent verification, native-versus-protected latency

27
00:02:04.332 --> 00:02:05.995
and cost accounting,

28
00:02:06.335 --> 00:02:10.038
negative tests for replay and mismatched artifacts,

29
00:02:10.038 --> 00:02:15.327
and a no-authorization non-claim. Choose among FHE, MPC, zero-knowledge proofs,

30
00:02:15.327 --> 00:02:18.501
confidential execution, private retrieval, differential privacy,

31
00:02:18.501 --> 00:02:23.790
or hybrid composition against an explicit adversary and leakage model.

32
00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:29.554
Bind code, model, configuration, data commitments, platform state, nonce or epoch,

33
00:02:29.554 --> 00:02:34.485
and policy identity into evidence that a distinct verifier appraises.

34
00:02:34.825 --> 00:02:39.677
Keep cryptographic correctness, attested identity, model quality, semantic validity,

35
00:02:39.677 --> 00:02:44.530
user authorization, and lawful purpose as separate claim classes.

36
00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:50.018
The design can still fail. Guarantee laundering collapses confidentiality,

37
00:02:50.018 --> 00:02:54.906
